# Exsecutor source holds itself to §8.1: UTF-8, no BOM, LF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# --- vendor/: third-party, byte-exact, DO NOT NORMALISE ---
#
# vendor/fasmg-x86/ is upstream fasmg's x86 macro package, vendored verbatim.
# Upstream ships CRLF; 71 of its 72 files contain CR. That is a deliberate
# exception to the LF rule above, not an oversight.
#
# The whole reason vendoring is defensible is that the tree is byte-identical
# to upstream and provable by hash (see vendor/fasmg-x86/PROVENANCE.md). If git
# normalised these line endings on checkout -- which it would on any clone with
# core.autocrlf=true -- the recorded digest would no longer reproduce and the
# re-vendor diff would show every file as changed. `-text` disables all EOL
# conversion in both directions.
vendor/fasmg-x86/** -text

# vendor/hydramodem-tx/ is HydraModem's reference-transmitter WAV output and
# the symbol streams reduced from it, vendored as a test certificate (see
# vendor/hydramodem-tx/PROVENANCE.md). Binary, not source -- `-text`
# disables git's EOL conversion and diffing so the bytes (and therefore the
# recorded per-file and tree sha256 digests) never move on checkout, the same
# reason vendor/fasmg-x86/** is pinned above.
vendor/hydramodem-tx/** -text

# --- tests/conformance/: byte-exact by definition, DO NOT NORMALISE ---
#
# These fixtures exist to violate §8.1 on purpose. entry20_crlf.exsc must
# CONTAIN a CRLF or it tests nothing, and `* text=auto eol=lf` above would
# strip it on commit -- leaving a fixture that passes for the wrong reason on
# every clone. Exactly the vendor/fasmg-x86 problem: a file whose whole point
# is its bytes, normalised by a rule written for source.
#
# Scoped to the whole directory rather than the two files that need it today,
# because the next fixture that needs byte-exactness will be written by
# someone who has not read this comment.
#
# Caught when entry18_bom.exsc turned out to contain no BOM at all -- it was
# written with a heredoc that never emitted one, so `exsc` correctly accepted
# it and the entry had been testing nothing.
tests/conformance/** -text

# --- tests/programs/*/expected.out: a program's stdout, byte-exact ---
#
# tests/run.sh compares a program's stdout to this file with `cmp`. It is not
# source and need not be text: tests/programs/octeti/expected.out holds 0x00,
# 0xff and lone UTF-8 lead and continuation bytes, because that program writes
# raw bytes (Scriptor.scribe_octeto). `text=auto` would detect that one as
# binary by its NUL, but the next reference may hold a CR and no NUL, and
# `eol=lf` would then rewrite the expectation instead of the program failing.
tests/programs/*/expected.out -text

# --- tests/data/*.bin: a fixture's INPUT bytes, byte-exact ---
#
# The same rule for the other direction. tests/run.sh's `stdin=` points a
# fixture's fd 0 at one of these (tests/data/lector_intra.bin holds 0x00,
# 0xff, a lone 0x80 and a CR; tests/data/lector_quattuor.bin is four bytes and
# no NUL-free heuristic would call it text), and for the `cat` program the
# same file is ALSO the expected stdout -- so a checkout that rewrote a CR
# would change the input and the expectation together and the test would pass
# while proving nothing. `-text`, for exactly the reason above.
tests/data/*.bin -text

# examples/hydramodem/loopback.wav is what the transmitter in that directory
# writes -- audio, and a WAV header whose fields a CR would ruin. It is
# byte-identical to the vendored reference render it is certified against
# (vendor/hydramodem-tx/d310123400a1ffffdeadbeef0a1b2ca961.wav); it is here so
# that a reader can hear the thing without building anything. `-text`.
examples/hydramodem/*.wav -text

# PROVENANCE.md is ours, not upstream's, and is excluded from the digest.
vendor/fasmg-x86/PROVENANCE.md text eol=lf
vendor/hydramodem-tx/PROVENANCE.md text eol=lf
